  our government is really uh the illness  and  and so i think it's something that we're  going to have to continue  to work on and i must say that i'm proud  of the effort  that we made with the problem solvers  caucus to keep pushing  and really we were ranking rank and file  members who came to the table  to continue to push for this package and  you know i'm hopeful under this  under president-elect biden that this  type of brinkmanship  in this ultra divisiveness is going to  change and we're going to see  a new period of trying to cooperate and  compromise and work together  now when you compromise obviously you  know  not everyone's going to be 100 happy  with the product but  ultimately that's what gets the job done  we put that entire interview on eight  news now  dot com our washington dc bureau  correspondent alexandra lamone has  stayed on top of these relief package  negotiations  i asked her about what we can all expect  now  lawmakers say they are once again on the  brink of a kovic 19 relief  deal our washington correspondent  alexandra lamone has more on that  alex why has this process been so drawn  out  you know there were a few different  issues that were holding up the  negotiations but one of the biggest  things  was the unemployment benefits we know  that democrats have been pushing for a  long time to both  extend and increase the amount of  federal unemployment benefits  that people get but for a long time  republicans were really trying to push  back on that in fact some republicans  didn't want any  additional unemployment benefits at all  but in the end they did agree  to some extension you know it was for a  shorter time  than democrats wanted and also for less  money and instead at the very last  minute what they said instead of doing  these  longer and larger unemployment benefits  what they wanted to do republicans would  send out  another round of those one-time stimulus  checks to americans this came up at the  very last minute  because for you know weeks now these  negotiations have been going on and they  didn't  include stimulus checks and that caught  some democrats off guard  in fact uh senator mark warner virginia  said  you know he was pretty upset about it he  said it didn't make sense  money to americans who are employed  rather than sending more money to people  who lost their jobs who may be on the  brink of losing their homes  and who maybe don't even have you know  the money to feed their families and so  that was one of the issues that really  held up negotiations  at the very end and that's in addition  to the things that have been ongoing  like whether or not to provide more  money to stay in local governments  where do you expect this will go now so  lawmakers are now saying they do  expect to be able to reach a deal  by this weekend initially the deadline  was friday  but now they're talking about extending  the budget you know for  one or two days worth of funding um so  that they can hold that larger budget  deal  and tie the stimulus package to it and  pass it then so it is  possible that we could see that happen  this weekend despite these last-minute  disagreements they say they are still  hopeful that can happen  that was alexandra lamone from our  washington dc bureau  the 2021 legislative session begins in  february and we are approaching  900 bill draft requests already  on a wide variety of subjects nearly 200  of them have been  written so this week we want to  highlight some of those  in the assembly assembly bill 10 would  double the criminal penalties for  vehicular manslaughter  right now it's a maximum of six months  and a thousand dollar  fine it would go up to 364 days and a 2  000 fine it is sponsored by the  judiciary committee  assembly bill 20 would revive the film  tax credit for  companies that shoot movies in nevada  the film tax credit was initially the  baby of  now attorney general aaron ford when he  was the senate majority leader in 2013.  it was initially 80 million dollars in  transferable credits  the legislature gutted it to 10 million  dollars a year later and  gave the rest to tesla during a special  session  it was brought back again in 2017 but  became part of the education budget deal  in 2019 the latest version is sponsored  by the committee on revenue  ab39 would hide more records from the  public  it would make notes and working papers  that are deemed for personal use not  applicable to public records requests  that is from the committee on government  affairs  ab56 would require high school students  to take a class on the holocaust  or the history of genocides this one is  from the education committee  and assembly bill 60 targets  non-disclosure agreements  it voids any part of a contract or  agreement that requires someone to not  testify in court  about criminal conduct or sexual  harassment  that one is from the judiciary committee  the 2021 legislature starts on february  1st in carson city  they have not yet said what said what  kind of coveted precautions will be  in place here's what we're keeping an  eye on next week in what to watch on  monday clark county is holding a virtual  town hall on the eviction moratorium  and rental assistance it's meant to help  both those facing eviction  and landlords who are not receiving  their normal  rent thanks for watching politics now
 

